   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Generative vs. Discriminative Models\n",
    "\n",
    "### What we've done so far (discriminative)\n",
    "\n",
    "Every algorithm in L01–L04 modeled $p(y \\mid x; \\theta)$ directly:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Algorithm | Modeled distribution |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Linear regression | $y \\mid x \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\theta^T x, \\sigma^2)$ |\n",
    "| Logistic regression | $y \\mid x \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\sigma(\\theta^T x))$ |\n",
    "| GLMs (general) | $y \\mid x$ in exponential family with $\\eta = \\theta^T x$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key insight:** given $x$, estimate parameters, decide class. Never explicitly model the data distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The generative idea\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of modeling $p(y \\mid x)$, model the **joint** $p(x, y) = p(x \\mid y)\\, p(y)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then classify via Bayes' rule:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(y \\mid x) = \\frac{p(x \\mid y)\\, p(y)}{p(x)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two components to learn:**\n",
    "- $p(x \\mid y)$ — class-conditional density: \"what do cat features look like?\"\n",
    "- $p(y)$ — class prior: \"how many cats vs. elephants are there?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"In the generative approach, what we're going to do instead of finding that boundary is we're going to model each class effectively independently. We'll ask instead: what do the features look like for every class?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why discriminative?\n",
    "\n",
    "If classes are easy to separate, you don't need to model their full distributions — just the boundary. Discriminative models devote all capacity to the decision surface.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why generative?\n",
    "\n",
    "If you model how data is *generated*, you can:\n",
    "- Generate new samples (diffusion models, LLMs)\n",
    "- Be more sample-efficient when the generative assumption is correct\n",
    "- Build richer representations of the world\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"The G in GPT stands for Generative. It trains on huge amounts of unsupervised text during pre-training. It tries to predict the next token, which brings a bunch of representations about it. It is a generative model underneath the covers.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 🎯 **Interview:** What is the difference between a generative and a discriminative model?\n",
    "> \n",
    "> **A:** A discriminative model learns $p(y \\mid x)$ — the boundary between classes. A generative model learns $p(x \\mid y)$ and $p(y)$ — the full data distribution for each class — then uses Bayes' rule to classify. Discriminative models are often more accurate when data is plentiful. Generative models can generate new samples, work with less labeled data, and enable pre-training (as in GPT).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Multivariate Gaussian Review\n",
    "\n",
    "GDA assumes $p(x \\mid y)$ is Gaussian. We need the multivariate version.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1D Gaussian (recall)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\sigma^2) \\implies p(z) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\sigma} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(z-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Multivariate Gaussian\n",
    "\n",
    "For $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\Sigma) \\implies p(x) = \\frac{1}{(2\\pi)^{d/2}|\\Sigma|^{1/2}} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu)^T \\Sigma^{-1} (x-\\mu)\\right)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Parameters:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Symbol | Name | Role |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| $\\mu \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ | Mean vector | Center of the distribution |\n",
    "| $\\Sigma \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$ | Covariance matrix | Shape, size, orientation of ellipses |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Properties of $\\Sigma$\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Symmetric:** $\\Sigma = \\Sigma^T$\n",
    "- **Positive definite:** all eigenvalues $\\lambda_i > 0$\n",
    "  - Ensures $|\\Sigma| > 0$ (determinant defined, normalization valid)\n",
    "  - Ensures $\\Sigma^{-1}$ exists (Mahalanobis distance well-defined)\n",
    "  - Ellipses are non-degenerate (no flat dimensions)\n",
    "- **Diagonal entries:** always positive (individual variances)\n",
    "- **Off-diagonal entries:** can be negative (negative correlation)\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Positive definite does not mean all entries are positive. Diagonal entries have to be positive, yes — but off-diagonal entries can be negative. Positive definite means all eigenvalues are positive.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### $(x-\\mu)^T \\Sigma^{-1} (x-\\mu)$ — Mahalanobis distance\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a distance metric weighted by $\\Sigma^{-1}$. Level sets of equal Mahalanobis distance form ellipses whose shape is controlled by $\\Sigma$.\n",
    "\n",
    "| $\\Sigma$ form | Contour shape |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| $\\Sigma = I$ | Perfect circles |\n",
    "| $\\Sigma$ diagonal | Axis-aligned ellipses |\n",
    "| $\\Sigma$ full | Rotated ellipses |\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Changing $\\mu$ shifts the center and the shape stays the same. $\\Sigma$ controls the shape, size, and orientation of the Gaussian contours.\"\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from matplotlib.patches import Ellipse\n",
    "\n",
    "def plot_gaussian_contours(mu, Sigma, ax, color='blue', label='', n_std=2):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Plot ellipse contours of a 2D Gaussian.\"\"\"\n",
    "    # Eigendecomposition gives rotation and scale\n",
    "    vals, vecs = np.linalg.eigh(Sigma)\n",
    "    # Angle of the first eigenvector\n",
    "    angle = np.degrees(np.arctan2(vecs[1, 0], vecs[0, 0]))\n",
    "    for s in range(1, n_std + 1):\n",
    "        width = 2 * s * np.sqrt(vals[0])\n",
    "        height = 2 * s * np.sqrt(vals[1])\n",
    "        ell = Ellipse(xy=mu, width=width, height=height, angle=angle,\n",
    "                      edgecolor=color, facecolor='none', linewidth=1.5,\n",
    "                      label=label if s == 1 else None)\n",
    "        ax.add_patch(ell)\n",
    "    ax.plot(*mu, marker='*', color=color, markersize=12)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(14, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "configs = [\n",
    "    (\"Identity (circular)\",  np.array([[1, 0], [0, 1]])),\n",
    "    (\"Diagonal (axis-aligned)\", np.array([[3, 0], [0, 0.5]])),\n",
    "    (\"Full (rotated)\",       np.array([[2, 1.5], [1.5, 2]])),\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "mu = np.array([0, 0])\n",
    "for ax, (title, Sigma) in zip(axes, configs):\n",
    "    plot_gaussian_contours(mu, Sigma, ax, color='steelblue')\n",
    "    lim = 4\n",
    "    ax.set_xlim(-lim, lim); ax.set_ylim(-lim, lim)\n",
    "    ax.set_aspect('equal'); ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'Σ = {title}', fontsize=10)\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('x₁'); ax.set_ylabel('x₂')\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Multivariate Gaussian — Effect of Σ on Contour Shape', fontsize=12)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Key insight: same Σ → same shape ellipses, different μ → shifted centers.\")\n",
    "print(\"GDA uses shared Σ: both classes get the same ellipse shape.\")"
   ]
  },
