    "## 3. The GDA Model\n",
    "\n",
    "**Setting:** Binary classification, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$, $y \\in \\{0, 1\\}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Generative assumptions:**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\phi)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x \\mid y=0 \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_0,\\, \\Sigma)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x \\mid y=1 \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_1,\\, \\Sigma)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key assumption:** both classes share the *same* covariance $\\Sigma$ but have *different* means $\\mu_0 \\ne \\mu_1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Parameters to learn:** $\\{\\phi,\\, \\mu_0,\\, \\mu_1,\\, \\Sigma\\}$\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"The two non-trivial things are: the means are different, and we allow the population sizes to be different — and that's the whole model.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why shared $\\Sigma$?\n",
    "\n",
    "- Fewer parameters to estimate: $\\Sigma \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$ has $d(d+1)/2$ free entries (symmetric)\n",
    "- For high-dimensional $x$ (large $d$), fitting separate covariances is expensive and requires more data\n",
    "- Leads to a **linear** decision boundary (key result below)\n",
    "- Relaxing this gives **Quadratic Discriminant Analysis (QDA)**\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparing parameter counts\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Parameters |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Logistic regression | $d + 1$ (just $\\theta$) |\n",
    "| GDA | $1 + d + d + d(d+1)/2 = O(d^2)$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "GDA models much more structure — it has to, because it models the full data distribution.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. MLE for GDA — Closed-Form Estimators\n",
    "\n",
    "### The joint log-likelihood\n",
    "\n",
    "Given training data $\\{(x^{(i)}, y^{(i)})\\}_{i=1}^n$, maximize the joint log-likelihood:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\ell(\\phi, \\mu_0, \\mu_1, \\Sigma) = \\log \\prod_{i=1}^n p(x^{(i)}, y^{(i)})\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "By IID assumption and Bayes' rule:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "= \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[\\log p(x^{(i)} \\mid y^{(i)}) + \\log p(y^{(i)})\\right]\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Taking derivatives and setting to zero yields **closed-form** solutions — no gradient descent needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Something magical happens — we can set the derivatives to zero and solve it and get a closed form solution. We didn't have to compute any gradient descent to do this. We just had to add it up.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### MLE estimates\n",
    "\n",
    "**Class prior** (fraction of class 1 examples):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\phi} = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = 1\\}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Class means** (average features within each class):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\mu}_0 = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = 0\\}\\, x^{(i)}}{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = 0\\}}\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\hat{\\mu}_1 = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = 1\\}\\, x^{(i)}}{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = 1\\}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Shared covariance** (pooled across both classes):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\Sigma} = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(x^{(i)} - \\hat{\\mu}_{y^{(i)}}\\right)\\left(x^{(i)} - \\hat{\\mu}_{y^{(i)}}\\right)^T\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\hat{\\mu}_{y^{(i)}}$ is the mean of whichever class example $i$ belongs to.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Derivation sketch for $\\hat{\\mu}_1$\n",
    "\n",
    "The only term in $\\ell$ that depends on $\\mu_1$ is $\\sum_{i: y^{(i)}=1} \\log p(x^{(i)} \\mid y^{(i)}=1)$. Taking $\\nabla_{\\mu_1}$ of the Gaussian log-likelihood:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\nabla_{\\mu_1} \\ell = \\Sigma^{-1} \\sum_{i: y^{(i)}=1} (x^{(i)} - \\mu_1) = 0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Since $\\Sigma^{-1}$ is invertible (positive definite), the only solution is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sum_{i: y^{(i)}=1} (x^{(i)} - \\mu_1) = 0 \\implies \\hat{\\mu}_1 = \\frac{\\sum_{i: y^{(i)}=1} x^{(i)}}{|\\{i : y^{(i)} = 1\\}|}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"This is just the world's most obfuscated way you've ever seen to compute the average. That's what we're doing — take all the points, add them up, divide them. All the cats. All the elephants. Clear enough in the notation.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 🎯 **Interview:** Why does GDA have closed-form MLE estimates while logistic regression requires iterative optimization?\n",
    "> \n",
    "> **A:** GDA's log-likelihood separates into independent terms for $\\phi$, $\\mu_0$, $\\mu_1$, and $\\Sigma$ — each quadratic (for means) or linear (for prior) with a unique global maximum that can be solved analytically. Logistic regression maximizes $\\log p(y \\mid x; \\theta)$ which involves the sigmoid — the resulting gradient equation has no closed-form solution, requiring iterative Newton's method or SGD.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from matplotlib.patches import Ellipse\n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(42)\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- GDA: fit parameters ---\n",
    "\n",
    "def fit_gda(X, y):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Fit GDA model. Returns phi, mu0, mu1, Sigma.\"\"\"\n",
    "    n = len(y)\n",
    "    idx0 = y == 0\n",
    "    idx1 = y == 1\n",
    "\n",
    "    phi = idx1.sum() / n                   # class prior: fraction of class 1\n",
    "    mu0 = X[idx0].mean(axis=0)             # mean of class 0\n",
    "    mu1 = X[idx1].mean(axis=0)             # mean of class 1\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Pooled covariance (shared Sigma)\n",
    "    mu_per_sample = np.where(y[:, None] == 0, mu0, mu1)  # (n, d)\n",
    "    diffs = X - mu_per_sample                             # (n, d)\n",
    "    Sigma = (diffs.T @ diffs) / n                         # (d, d)\n",
    "\n",
    "    return phi, mu0, mu1, Sigma\n",
    "\n",
    "def gda_predict(X, phi, mu0, mu1, Sigma):\n",
    "    \"\"\"GDA prediction via Bayes rule: argmax_y p(x|y) p(y).\"\"\"\n",
    "    Sigma_inv = np.linalg.inv(Sigma)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def log_class_prob(X, mu, prior):\n",
    "        # log p(x|y) = -0.5 (x-mu)^T Sigma^{-1} (x-mu) + const\n",
    "        diff = X - mu          # (n, d)\n",
    "        maha = (diff @ Sigma_inv * diff).sum(axis=1)  # Mahalanobis^2\n",
    "        return -0.5 * maha + np.log(prior)\n",
    "\n",
    "    log_p0 = log_class_prob(X, mu0, 1 - phi)\n",
    "    log_p1 = log_class_prob(X, mu1, phi)\n",
    "    return (log_p1 > log_p0).astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Generate toy data (cats=0, elephants=1)\n",
    "mu_cat = np.array([1.0, 1.0])\n",
    "mu_elephant = np.array([4.5, 4.5])\n",
    "Sigma_true = np.array([[1.2, 0.5], [0.5, 1.2]])\n",
    "\n",
    "n_cat, n_elephant = 80, 70\n",
    "X_cat = np.random.multivariate_normal(mu_cat, Sigma_true, n_cat)\n",
    "X_elephant = np.random.multivariate_normal(mu_elephant, Sigma_true, n_elephant)\n",
    "X = np.vstack([X_cat, X_elephant])\n",
    "y = np.array([0] * n_cat + [1] * n_elephant)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Fit\n",
    "phi, mu0_hat, mu1_hat, Sigma_hat = fit_gda(X, y)\n",
    "print(f\"phi (fraction class 1): {phi:.3f}  (true: {n_elephant/(n_cat+n_elephant):.3f})\")\n",
    "print(f\"mu0 (cats):     {mu0_hat}  (true: {mu_cat})\")\n",
    "print(f\"mu1 (elephants):{mu1_hat}  (true: {mu_elephant})\")\n",
    "print(f\"Sigma estimated:\\n{np.round(Sigma_hat, 3)}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Sigma true:\\n{Sigma_true}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Accuracy\n",
    "y_pred = gda_predict(X, phi, mu0_hat, mu1_hat, Sigma_hat)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nTraining accuracy: {(y_pred == y).mean():.3f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Visualize GDA fit with decision boundary\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 7))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Scatter data\n",
    "ax.scatter(X_cat[:, 0], X_cat[:, 1], c='orange', marker='o', label='Cats (class 0)', alpha=0.7, edgecolors='k', s=40)\n",
    "ax.scatter(X_elephant[:, 0], X_elephant[:, 1], c='gray', marker='s', label='Elephants (class 1)', alpha=0.7, edgecolors='k', s=40)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot ellipse contours (same Sigma, different mu)\n",
    "def add_ellipse(ax, mu, Sigma, color, label):\n",
    "    vals, vecs = np.linalg.eigh(Sigma)\n",
    "    angle = np.degrees(np.arctan2(vecs[1, 0], vecs[0, 0]))\n",
    "    for s in [1, 2]:\n",
    "        ell = Ellipse(xy=mu, width=2*s*np.sqrt(vals[0]), height=2*s*np.sqrt(vals[1]),\n",
    "                      angle=angle, edgecolor=color, facecolor='none', linewidth=2,\n",
    "                      label=label if s == 1 else None)\n",
    "        ax.add_patch(ell)\n",
    "    ax.plot(*mu, marker='*', color=color, markersize=16, zorder=5)\n",
    "\n",
    "add_ellipse(ax, mu0_hat, Sigma_hat, 'darkorange', 'Cat Gaussian (1σ, 2σ)')\n",
    "add_ellipse(ax, mu1_hat, Sigma_hat, 'dimgray', 'Elephant Gaussian (1σ, 2σ)')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Draw decision boundary (where log-odds = 0)\n",
    "xx, yy = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(-1, 7, 300), np.linspace(-1, 7, 300))\n",
    "grid = np.c_[xx.ravel(), yy.ravel()]\n",
    "preds = gda_predict(grid, phi, mu0_hat, mu1_hat, Sigma_hat).reshape(xx.shape)\n",
    "ax.contour(xx, yy, preds, levels=[0.5], colors='red', linewidths=2, linestyles='--')\n",
    "ax.plot([], [], 'r--', linewidth=2, label='GDA decision boundary (linear)')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(-1, 7); ax.set_ylim(-1, 7)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Feature 1 (weight)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Feature 2 (height)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax.set_title('GDA: Shared Σ → Linear Decision Boundary', fontsize=13)\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=9)\n",
    "ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Note: both ellipses have the SAME shape (shared Sigma).\")\n",
    "print(\"The red dashed line is the linear decision boundary.\")"
   ]
  },
