    "## 6. GDA vs. Logistic Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "### The surprising connection\n",
    "\n",
    "After deriving the GDA decision rule via Bayes, we can rewrite $p(y=1 \\mid x)$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(y=1 \\mid x) = \\frac{p(x \\mid y=1)\\, p(y=1)}{p(x \\mid y=1)\\,p(y=1) + p(x \\mid y=0)\\,p(y=0)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "When we expand using Gaussian log-likelihoods with shared $\\Sigma$, this becomes:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(y=1 \\mid x) = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-\\theta^T x - \\theta_0}} = \\sigma(\\theta^T x + \\theta_0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\theta = \\Sigma^{-1}(\\mu_1 - \\mu_0)$ and $\\theta_0$ is a function of $\\mu_0, \\mu_1, \\Sigma, \\phi$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**This is exactly logistic regression's functional form.** GDA implies logistic regression.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"GDA implies that logistic regression — in fancy terminology — has this same sigmoidal form as the posterior distribution. It's not just Gaussians; it's also other exponential families. Many different generative assumptions on your data lead to the same discriminative model: logistic regression.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "| | GDA | Logistic Regression |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Assumption** | $p(x|y)$ is Gaussian, shared $\\Sigma$ | Log-odds is linear in $x$ |\n",
    "| **Boundary** | Linear (same as LR when GDA assumptions hold) | Linear |\n",
    "| **Parameters** | $O(d^2)$ | $O(d)$ |\n",
    "| **Data efficiency** | More efficient if Gaussian assumption correct | Needs more data |\n",
    "| **Robustness** | Fragile if assumption wrong | More robust |\n",
    "| **Implication** | GDA $\\Rightarrow$ LR form | LR does **not** $\\Rightarrow$ GDA |\n",
    "\n",
    "**GDA is a strictly stronger assumption.** If GDA holds, logistic regression also works. But logistic regression can find the right decision line even when data is *not* Gaussian.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Logistic regression assumes less and still gets the right answer for these kinds of problems. But then you would have given up on all this amazing AI richness over the last 10 years — generating text, generating images. That line of thinking of 'let's model the data' led to it.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### The modern perspective\n",
    "\n",
    "Historically, logistic regression won out because it's robust. But the biggest advances in AI came from the **generative** direction:\n",
    "- **Diffusion models** (Stable Diffusion, DALL-E): model $p(x)$ via denoising\n",
    "- **LLMs** (GPT): generative pre-training on text tokens\n",
    "- **GANs**: generator + discriminator, explicitly generative\n",
    "\n",
    "GDA at lecture 5 is the seed of this entire lineage.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 🎯 **Interview:** Why does GDA imply logistic regression but not vice versa?\n",
    "> \n",
    "> **A:** GDA makes a stronger assumption — that class-conditional densities are Gaussian with shared covariance. Under this assumption, Bayes' rule produces a posterior $p(y=1|x)$ that has exactly the sigmoid form of logistic regression. So any GDA model *is* a logistic regression with specific parameters. But logistic regression only assumes the log-odds is linear — it doesn't require Gaussian data. There are non-Gaussian datasets where logistic regression finds the right boundary but GDA fails.\n"
   ]
