    "## 7. Naive Bayes\n",
    "\n",
    "GDA handles **continuous** features. For **discrete** features (e.g., words in a document), we need a different model.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The curse of dimensionality for discrete features\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose $x \\in \\{0,1\\}^d$ (binary word presence vector) with $d = 50{,}000$ words.\n",
    "\n",
    "The full joint $p(x \\mid y)$ requires a table with $2^d$ entries — more than atoms in the observable universe. Completely intractable.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"With D binary features, X could take $2^D$ possible values. If D has 50,000 words, we're looking at $2^{50,000}$. That's like the number of atoms in the observable universe. We're toast. We cannot represent this.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### The Naive Bayes assumption\n",
    "\n",
    "**Assume features are conditionally independent given the class:**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_d \\mid y) = \\prod_{j=1}^d p(x_j \\mid y)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is called **Naive Bayes** because the independence assumption is naive (words in a sentence are obviously not independent), yet the model works surprisingly well in practice.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Parameter reduction:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| | Full model | Naive Bayes |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Parameters for $p(x|y)$ | $2^d$ | $2d$ (one per feature per class) |\n",
    "| Growth rate | Exponential in $d$ | Linear in $d$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Bernoulli Naive Bayes (binary features)\n",
    "\n",
    "Each feature $x_j \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ is a Bernoulli:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(x_j = 1 \\mid y = k) = \\phi_{j|k}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**MLE estimates** (just counting):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\phi}_y = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = 1\\}}{n}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\phi}_{j|k} = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{x_j^{(i)} = 1,\\, y^{(i)} = k\\}}{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = k\\}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Prediction via Bayes' rule** (in log domain for numerical stability):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{y} = \\arg\\max_k \\left[\\log p(y=k) + \\sum_{j=1}^d \\log p(x_j \\mid y=k)\\right]\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"How do you estimate how many spam emails contain the word 'buy'? You look at all the spam emails, see how many contain the word, and divide. That's your fraction. And all of these are maximum likelihood estimates. It's just like GDA. It's dirt cheap to train even on a huge corpus. And it's surprisingly accurate.\"\n"
   ]
