    "## 8. Laplace Smoothing\n",
    "\n",
    "### The zero-count problem\n",
    "\n",
    "If a word $j$ never appears in training spam emails, the MLE gives:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\phi}_{j|\\text{spam}} = 0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At test time, if a spam email contains that word:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(x \\mid y=\\text{spam}) \\propto \\cdots \\times 0 \\times \\cdots = 0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The model assigns zero probability to spam — killing all evidence regardless of other words. One unseen word ruins the entire prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Laplace smoothing\n",
    "\n",
    "Add a pseudocount of 1 to every feature count:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\phi}_{j|k} = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{x_j^{(i)} = 1,\\, y^{(i)} = k\\}\\right) + 1}{\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)} = k\\}\\right) + 2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For a vocabulary of size $V$ (multinomial NB), add $V$ pseudocounts to the denominator:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\phi}_{j|k} = \\frac{\\text{count}(j, k) + 1}{\\text{count}(k) + V}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Effect:**\n",
    "- No feature ever gets probability 0\n",
    "- Shrinks confidence on rarely-seen features toward the prior\n",
    "- Equivalent to a Dirichlet prior (Bayesian interpretation)\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Laplace smoothing prevents zero probabilities and shrinks your confidence in estimates. It's a very simple smoothing form that gives appreciable results. You can use other numbers there too. We'll talk about various forms of regularization that basically make the model less confident about what it's seeing.\"\n"
   ]
