    "## Summary — Big Picture\n",
    "\n",
    "### Two families of classifiers\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "Discriminative                    Generative\n",
    "─────────────────                 ────────────────────\n",
    "Model: p(y | x)                   Model: p(x | y) · p(y)\n",
    "\n",
    "Logistic regression               GDA (continuous x)\n",
    "Linear regression                 Naive Bayes (discrete x)\n",
    "SVM, decision trees               VAE, GAN, diffusion, GPT\n",
    "\n",
    "Weak assumption                   Stronger assumption\n",
    "Needs more data                   More efficient with less data\n",
    "More robust                       Fragile if assumption wrong\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### L05 Derivations\n",
    "\n",
    "| Result | Formula | Method |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Class prior | $\\hat{\\phi} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum \\mathbf{1}\\{y^{(i)}=1\\}$ | MLE closed-form |\n",
    "| Class means | $\\hat{\\mu}_k = \\text{avg}(x^{(i)} : y^{(i)}=k)$ | MLE closed-form |\n",
    "| Shared covariance | $\\hat{\\Sigma} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum (x^{(i)}-\\hat{\\mu}_{y^{(i)}})(x^{(i)}-\\hat{\\mu}_{y^{(i)}})^T$ | MLE closed-form |\n",
    "| Decision boundary | $\\theta^T x = \\theta_0$ (hyperplane) | Quadratic terms cancel |\n",
    "| NB feature prob | $\\hat{\\phi}_{j|k} = \\frac{\\text{count}(j,k)+1}{\\text{count}(k)+2}$ | MLE + Laplace |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Connection to modern AI\n",
    "\n",
    "| Topic today | Modern descendant |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Generative models | GPT, diffusion, DALL-E, Gemini |\n",
    "| Class-conditional Gaussians | VAE encoder/decoder |\n",
    "| Feature independence (NB) | Attention as soft feature selection |\n",
    "| Bayes' rule classification | Posterior sampling in LLMs |\n",
    "| Laplace smoothing | Label smoothing (L04), dropout, weight decay |\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Don't be worried that GDA doesn't do that well on linear kinds of problems. It shines much later, and that's why we teach it. The line of thinking — 'let's model the data' — led to all of it.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 🎯 **Interview:** What is the key assumption of Naive Bayes and why does it matter?\n",
    "> \n",
    "> **A:** Naive Bayes assumes features are conditionally independent given the class: $p(x_1, \\ldots, x_d | y) = \\prod_j p(x_j | y)$. This is almost always wrong (words in text co-occur, pixels in images are correlated), but it reduces the parameter space from $O(2^d)$ to $O(d)$, making the model tractable. Despite the naive assumption, it works remarkably well in practice — especially for text classification — because it only needs to model the decision boundary well, not the full data distribution.\n",
